from pathlib import Path
p=Path('research/labour-force-survey-uncertainty-measurement.html'); h=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
imputation=r'''<section class="chapter" id="imputation"><span class="part">Processing · missing information is also uncertain</span><h2>Imputation, Missing Data, and the Statistical Consequences of Nonresponse</h2><p class="lead">A complete record can contain estimated answers. Completeness is not evidence that every field was observed or that its unknown truth has been recovered.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Separate the source of a gap from its treatment</caption><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Unit nonresponse</th><td>No usable interview information for a sampled household or person.</td><td>Known demographic/history information helps determine imputation versus weight adjustment.</td></tr><tr><th>Item nonresponse</th><td>An otherwise usable record lacks one or more answers, including “don't know” or refusal.</td><td>Item-level treatment may preserve the observed fields.</td></tr><tr><th>Invalid response</th><td>Outside permitted ranges or codes.</td><td>Flag for correction or replacement.</td></tr><tr><th>Inconsistent response</th><td>Conflicts with another answer or questionnaire logic.</td><td>Apply edits; unusual but possible responses are retained.</td></tr><tr><th>Edited value</th><td>A value changed through a validation or consistency rule.</td><td>Editing and imputation can overlap; an edit can create a missing field needing imputation.</td></tr><tr><th>Imputed value</th><td>A replacement generated using rules, prior answers or donor information.</td><td>Its validity is conditional on the method and available information.</td></tr><tr><th>Weight adjustment</th><td>A change in representation assigned to responding records.</td><td>It changes contributions to estimates; it does not supply the missing person's answer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The current LFS documents <strong>manual, deterministic, carry-forward and donor item imputation</strong>. Manual work addresses crucial or complicated demographics such as age or economic family structure. Completed records are checked again for validity and consistency. The 2017 hot-deck architecture explains matching and edit constraints in depth; its precise class variables and processing phases are historical. The 2025 guide notes a move to the Social Survey Processing Environment in 2019, the integrated collection platform in 2020, and imputation/outlier improvements in 2023.</p>
<h3>Deterministic imputation: information that implies an answer</h3><p>Write observed answers as \(x_i\), the missing field as y and a valid logical rule as \(g\). An illustrative rule for a jobholder with no actual work and a qualifying vacation absence is</p><div class="equation">\[\tilde y_i=\begin{cases}\text{employed, absent},&\text{job}_i=1,\ H_i=0,\ A_i=\text{vacation},\\\text{unresolved},&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}\]</div><p>For job=Yes, actual hours=0, absence=Vacation, this rule determines the status without borrowing a donor. It is a simplified application of official classification, not a rule that every zero-hour person is employed. The guide’s worked editing example reconciles a report of work with a later report of no hours to an employed-but-absent status. Wrong observed inputs can still propagate into a deterministic value.</p>
<h3>Carry-forward: borrow from the same person's past</h3><div class="equation">\[\tilde y_{i,t}=y_{i,t-1}\quad\text{when the prior value is valid and applicable}.\]</div><p>For example, an earlier usual-hours value of 35 can fill an unresolved current usual-hours field where the rule is appropriate. The 2025 guide names wages and usual work hours as common carry-forward fields. Persistence is a working assumption: actual hours and labour status may change rapidly. Carrying forward 35 when the unknown current value is 20 gives an error of 15; the old answer is not proof of the new one. Separately, the guide documents carrying initial labour responses for people aged 70+ through their subsequent five provincial interviews to reduce burden.</p>
<h3>Donor imputation: compatible observed records</h3><p>Define an illustrative donor class \(\mathcal D_i=\{k:R_k=1,\ C(x_k)=C(x_i),\ k\ne i\}\), where R indicates an observed valid value and C maps observed characteristics to a matching class. A selected compatible record \(d(i)\in\mathcal D_i\) supplies</p><div class="equation">\[\tilde y_i=y_{d(i)},\qquad y_i^{imp}=R_i y_i+(1-R_i)\tilde y_i.\]</div><p>In a teaching class with donor usual-hours values 30, 35 and 40, a uniformly selected donor has conditional expected value 35 and donor variance \((25+0+25)/3=16.67\). Receiving 35 says nothing definitive about the recipient’s true answer. The 2025 guide documents donors with similar characteristics. The 2017 §§5.3.1–5.3.2 describe random hot-deck selection within classes, donor eligibility, validity/consistency checks and collapsing sparse classes. Their exact historical variable order and minimum-donor rules are not asserted as the current implementation.</p>
<figure class="widget"><h3>Watch a donor value enter a missing field</h3><div class="donor-demo"><div>Observed donor<br>same teaching class<br><strong>Usual hours: 35</strong></div><span id="donor-transfer" aria-hidden="true">35 →</span><div>Recipient<br>reported job: Yes<br><strong id="donor-field">Usual hours: missing</strong></div></div><div class="controls"><button type="button" id="donor-run">Transfer donor value</button><button type="button" id="donor-reset">Reset example</button></div><p id="donor-status" role="status">The true recipient value remains unknown.</p><figcaption>Figure 4a. A compatible donor contributes one estimated answer; observed fields remain. This conceptual example omits the production matching and edit rules. Reduced-motion users receive an immediate update.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Whole-record donors and total nonresponse</h3><p>Under the 2025 guide, total nonresponse with known household demographics, usually from earlier collection, is treated by whole-record donor imputation. Matching uses previous-cycle labour information plus geography and demographics. If household demographics are unavailable, total nonresponse is handled by a nonresponse weight adjustment. Replacing a labour information vector \(\tilde{\mathbf y}_i=\mathbf y_{d(i)}\) is different from filling just one field. It preserves combinations of labour answers from a donor without establishing the recipient’s true combination. The 2017 §5.3.3 clarifies that donor and recipient records in its longitudinal system were person records even for household nonresponse.</p>
<h3>Imputation and weighting are related, distinct operations</h3><div class="equation">\[\hat T_{imp}=\sum_{i\in s} w_i\{R_i y_i+(1-R_i)\tilde y_i\},\qquad a_i=\frac{w_i^{(0)}}{\hat r_{g(i)}}.\]</div><p>The first equation describes an imputed total with the appropriate final weights; the second is a simple response-cell adjustment illustration, not the full LFS weighting algorithm. A response adjustment needs responding and missing outcomes to be sufficiently comparable within adjustment information. Calibration balances known auxiliary totals and composite information but cannot guarantee correction for differences within cells. The actual routing of cases determines which adjustments apply; multiplying every record by all adjustments indiscriminately can double count compensation. The <a href="#weights">next section</a> develops calibrated estimation.</p>
<h3>Bias, variance and assumptions about missingness</h3><p>For fixed weights and complete-data values, the discrepancy caused by item imputation is \(\hat T_{imp}-\hat T_{complete}=\sum_i w_i(1-R_i)(\tilde y_i-y_i)\). Its expectation is zero only under suitable assumptions about recipients, donors, logical rules or response processes. Missingness associated with the unobserved outcome can leave bias even after all fields are completed.</p><div class="equation">\[V(\hat T_{imp})=V_{s,R}\{E_D(\hat T_{imp}\mid s,R)\}+E_{s,R}\{V_D(\hat T_{imp}\mid s,R)\}.\]</div><p>This law-of-total-variance decomposition treats sampling s, response R and stochastic donor selection D as sources of randomness under a specified joint framework; conditioning includes available observed information. It is an analytical decomposition, not a claim that the LFS reports each component separately. Shared donors can create covariance; deterministic or carry-forward rules have no random donor-selection component but can still introduce errors and bias. Treating imputed values as independently and fully observed may underestimate uncertainty. Proper replication needs to reflect relevant imputation and weighting steps, or an appropriate model-based adjustment; the 2017 §7.3 describes limits on what its production replicates repeated.</p>
<details><summary>MCAR, MAR and MNAR: general analytical frameworks</summary><div><p>With X fully observed, \(Y_{obs}\) observed outcomes and \(Y_{mis}\) missing outcomes, <strong>MCAR</strong> means \(P(R\mid X,Y)=P(R)\). <strong>MAR</strong> means \(P(R\mid X,Y_{obs},Y_{mis})=P(R\mid X,Y_{obs})\). <strong>MNAR</strong> means residual dependence on \(Y_{mis}\) remains after observed information is conditioned upon. MAR alone is not enough: donor classes or outcome/response models must use relevant information and have overlap. These are general concepts, not assertions that a specific LFS procedure assumes one mechanism. MNAR generally requires sensitivity analysis or additional information.</p><p>Regression imputation and multiple imputation are possible teaching extensions, but are not claimed here as official LFS item-imputation methods. Multiple imputation can propagate specified model uncertainty across completed datasets; it still needs survey design, proper models and missingness assumptions. Neither it nor donor imputation removes all nonsampling error.</p></div></details>
<form class="widget" id="imputation-sim"><h3>Synthetic missing-data experiment</h3><span class="tag synthetic">Fixed complete dataset · equal weights · cell donors</span><div class="controls"><label>Missingness<select name="mechanism"><option value="MCAR">MCAR: 35% at random</option><option value="MAR">MAR: depends on age group</option><option value="MNAR">MNAR: depends on unemployment</option></select></label><label>Trials<input name="trials" type="number" min="100" max="2000" step="100" value="500"></label><label>Seed<input name="seed" type="number" min="1" max="999999" value="3701"></label><button type="submit">Run experiment</button><button type="reset">Reset</button></div><svg id="imputation-chart" class="chart" viewBox="0 0 800 280" role="img" aria-label="Mean complete, respondent-only and donor-imputed unemployment fractions across response trials"></svg><div id="imputation-result" class="result" aria-live="polite">Run to compare complete, missing and imputed data.</div><p class="small">200 synthetic labour-force members: 100 younger people, of whom 30 are unemployed, and 100 older people, of whom 10 are unemployed. Truth=20%. Response probability: MCAR 0.65; MAR 0.45 younger and 0.85 older; MNAR 0.35 unemployed and 0.85 employed. Donors are drawn uniformly from observed records in the same age group. Each trial regenerates response and donors, not the complete population. Bars show mean estimates; whiskers show ±1 empirical SD across trials, not confidence intervals. Empty donor cells are reported as failed trials. This compares methods under stated mechanisms, not actual LFS bias or precision.</p></form>
<p class="source"><a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/71-543-g/71-543-g2025001-eng.htm#a6">2025 Guide §6, pp. 25–28</a> (current methods and system changes); <a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/71-526-x/2017001/chapt5-eng.htm">2017 Chapter 5, §§5.1–5.4, pp. 35–39</a> (historical technical details). Equations and simulation are educational analysis.</p></section>'''
h=h.replace('<section class="chapter" id="weights">',imputation+'\n<section class="chapter" id="weights">',1)
h=h.replace('Bootstrap the survey structure, not independent people','Bootstrap Variance Estimation under Complex Survey Designs')
start=h.index('<details><summary>Python: synthetic stratified cluster bootstrap'); end=h.index('</details>',start)+len('</details>')
h=h[:start]+h[end:]
boot=r'''<h3>Ordinary iid bootstrap as a benchmark</h3><p>For n independent identically distributed observations, draw n indices independently with replacement from their empirical distribution. For a mean, \(\bar y^{(b)}=n^{-1}\sum_{r=1}^n y_{I_r^{(b)}}\). Conditional on the data, \(V_*(\bar y^{(b)})=n^{-2}\sum_i(y_i-\bar y)^2\), approximately \(s^2/n\). This is a useful iid benchmark, but independently redrawing survey persons breaks household/PSU dependence, stratum allocation and the connection between selection probabilities and weights.</p>
<h3>Replicate totals and ratios</h3><div class="equation">\[\hat T_y^{(b)}=\sum_{i\in s}w_i^{(b)}y_i,\qquad\hat R^{(b)}=\frac{\sum_{i\in s}w_i^{(b)}y_i}{\sum_{i\in s}w_i^{(b)}x_i},\qquad\widehat V=c\sum_{b=1}^B(\hat\theta^{(b)}-\hat\theta^\star)^2.\]</div><p>B counts replicates, \(\hat\theta^{(b)}\) is the statistic recomputed with replicate weights, \(\hat\theta^\star\) is the prescribed center and c is the scheme-specific multiplier. Some methods center at the original estimate; others at the replicate mean. Replicate-weight documentation determines both. A ratio needs both totals recalculated, with a nonzero replicate denominator. For a change, use the same coordinated replicate identifier across months before computing variance.</p>
<h3>Deriving a specified rescaled-cluster illustration</h3><p>Assume independent strata with \(n_h\ge2\) equally selected PSUs, negligible first-stage sampling fractions, and complete observed PSU summaries. Treat within-PSU contributions as fixed in this teaching approximation. Draw \(n_h-1\) sampled PSUs with replacement within each stratum. Multiplicities \(m_{hi}^{(b)}\) follow a multinomial distribution with \(n_h-1\) trials and equal probabilities \(1/n_h\). Let \(a_{hi}=\sum_j w_{hij}y_{hij}\) denote a weighted PSU contribution.</p><div class="equation">\[w_{hij}^{(b)}=\frac{n_h}{n_h-1}m_{hi}^{(b)}w_{hij},\quad E_*m_{hi}=\frac{n_h-1}{n_h},\quad V_*\!\left(\sum_i\frac{n_h}{n_h-1}m_{hi}a_{hi}\right)=\frac{n_h}{n_h-1}\sum_i(a_{hi}-\bar a_h)^2.\]</div><p>The expectation reproduces each original PSU contribution. Using multinomial variance and negative covariance yields the final expression, equal to the familiar with-replacement PSU variance estimate \(n_h s_{a,h}^2\). Independent strata add variance contributions. This derivation concerns totals; ratios use replicate recalculation or residual linearization. It does not establish validity for arbitrary PPS, large sampling fractions, certainty PSUs, lone-PSU strata or missing second-stage variation. Those cases need suitable design-specific adaptations. Here the Monte Carlo variance uses the replicate mean with c=1/B, matching the historical convention; c=1/(B−1) is the unbiased finite-B sample-variance alternative for independent simulated replicates.</p>
<figure class="widget"><h3>Unequal person weights in a small synthetic survey</h3><div id="replicate-data" class="table-wrap"></div><form id="replicate-sim"><div class="controls"><label>Replicates<input name="B" type="number" min="100" max="2000" step="100" value="1000"></label><label>Seed<input name="seed" type="number" min="1" max="999999" value="338"></label><button type="submit">Run design-aware replicates</button><button type="reset">Reset</button></div></form><svg id="replicate-chart" class="chart" viewBox="0 0 800 280" role="img" aria-label="Histogram of design-aware weighted ratio replicate estimates"></svg><div id="replicate-result" class="result" aria-live="polite">Run to inspect the original estimate and its replicates.</div><details><summary>First ten replicate calculations</summary><div id="replicate-table" class="table-wrap"></div></details><figcaption>Figure 6a. Two independent strata, four sampled PSUs each. Original PSU summaries appear above; base person weights differ between strata (10 versus 25), reflecting different sampling fractions. Resample three whole PSUs per stratum and use multiplicity ×4/3 ×original weight. No calibration, finite-population correction, nonresponse, random second-stage resampling or production LFS exceptions. The earlier <a href="#lab-f">Laboratory F</a> is retained as the equal-weight benchmark.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Confidence intervals: method and target must agree</h3><div class="equation">\[SE_{boot}=\sqrt{\widehat V},\qquad CI_{normal}=\hat\theta\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}SE_{boot},\qquad CI_{percentile}=[Q_{\alpha/2}(\hat\theta^{(b)}),Q_{1-\alpha/2}(\hat\theta^{(b)})].\]</div><p>A normal interval requires an adequately approximated sampling distribution, small bias and enough independent design information. Few PSUs, rare outcomes and unstable denominators can make it unreliable; a design-based t reference or another supported method may be preferable. The simulation reports a <em>nominal illustrative</em> 95% normal interval with no validated coverage claim for this eight-PSU sample.</p><p>Percentile endpoints summarize replicate quantiles and can reflect skewness, but rescaled replicate distributions are not automatically calibrated for percentile coverage. Bias, boundaries, transformations and the replication design matter. We show percentile quantiles only as descriptive endpoints, not an official LFS confidence interval. Published standard errors and design-supported approximations should guide real LFS inference.</p>
<details><summary>Python: reproduce the unequal-weight teaching scheme</summary><div><pre>import numpy as np
rng = np.random.default_rng(338)
U = np.array([[2, 4, 3, 7], [6, 10, 8, 12]])
L = np.array([[40, 50, 60, 50], [45, 55, 50, 60]])
w = np.array([[10], [25]])
original = (w * U).sum() / (w * L).sum()
replicates = []
for b in range(1000):
    m = np.array([np.bincount(rng.integers(0, 4, 3), minlength=4)
                  for h in range(2)])
    wb = w * (4 / 3) * m
    replicates.append((wb * U).sum() / (wb * L).sum())
v = np.mean((replicates - np.mean(replicates)) ** 2)
se = np.sqrt(v)
print(original, v, se, (original - 1.96*se, original + 1.96*se))</pre><p>This uses an equal-probability PSU approximation with unequal weights across strata. It does not recreate current LFS selection or final calibrated weights. NumPy and the browser use different seeded generators, so individual replicates differ. <a href="labour-force-survey-uncertainty-measurement/scripts/verify_enhancements.js">Browser-algorithm numerical checks</a> verify deterministic browser draws and compare variance against exact enumeration of this small teaching distribution.</p></div></details>
<p class="source">Historical bootstrap: <a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/71-526-x/2017001/chapt7-eng.htm">2017 Chapter 7, pp. 49–54, Eqs. 7.1–7.2</a>. Current availability and reporting: <a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/71-543-g/71-543-g2025001-eng.htm#a7">2025 Guide §7</a> and §9. The guide does not settle how every 2017 rescaling, imputation or redesign-transition rule is implemented after the 2025 redesign.</p>'''
pos=h.index('<section class="chapter" id="bootstrap">'); end=h.index('</section>',pos);h=h[:end]+boot+h[end:]
h=h.replace('<a href="#methodology">LFS methodology</a>','<a href="#survey-journey">Survey journey</a><a href="#methodology">LFS methodology</a><a href="#sampling-units">PSUs and SSUs</a><a href="#imputation">Imputation</a>',1)
h=h.replace('<a href="#overview">0 · Research overview</a>','<a href="#overview">0 · Research overview</a><a href="#survey-journey">Monthly survey journey</a><a href="#sampling-units">PSU and SSU construction</a><a href="#imputation">Imputation and missing data</a>',1)
h=h.replace('Conceptual population of 120 marks, with 12 sample marks highlighted','Conceptual population of 120 person pictograms, with 12 sampled people highlighted').replace('Animate sample marks','Animate sampled people').replace('Marks illustrate selection','Pictograms illustrate selection').replace('Selected clusters contain highlighted dwelling marks','Selected clusters contain highlighted dwelling pictograms')
p.write_text(h,encoding='utf-8')
# Change conceptual unit marks only; preserve quantitative SVG chart marks.
a=Path('research/labour-force-survey-uncertainty-measurement/js/app.js'); s=a.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
s=s.replace('aria-hidden="true"></span>','aria-hidden="true">🧍</span>')
s=s.replace("'Pause sample marks':'Animate sample marks'","'Pause sampled people':'Animate sampled people'")
s=s.replace('`<circle cx="${x+12+j*13}" cy="${y+24}" r="4" fill="${c===h&&j%2===0?\'#a54f24\':\'#91afa6\'}"/>`','`<text x="${x+6+j*13}" y="${y+30}" style="font-size:12px;opacity:${c===h&&j%2===0?1:.45}">🏡</text>`')
a.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
print('Expanded imputation and bootstrap; preserved existing anchors and quantitative charts.')
